2026-09-23T07:25:57.6389219Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6389491Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6389981Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6390374Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6390576Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6390827Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6391024Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6391251Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6391460Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6391723Z         def inc(x):
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6391941Z             return x + 1
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6392162Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6392344Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6392536Z         def test_answer():
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6392774Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6393007Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6393187Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6393370Z     To execute it::
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6393582Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6393775Z         $ pytest
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6394056Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6394420Z         collected 1 items
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6394777Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6394973Z         test_sample.py F
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6395202Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6395446Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6395863Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6396222Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6396413Z             def test_answer():
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6396671Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6396934Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6397178Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6397434Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6397643Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6397986Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6398326Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6398499Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6399477Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6400408Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6400579Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6400768Z     Features
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6400965Z     --------
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6401164Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6401732Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6402479Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6402672Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6403098Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6403596Z       of test modules and functions
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6403851Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6404226Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6404888Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6405223Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6405580Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6406045Z       test suites out of the box
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6406287Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6406486Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6406715Z     
2026-09-23T07:25:57.6407287Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
